  "query_preview": "..Baggy troughiness will persist across the desert \nsouthwest into the weekend. Late in the weekend into early next week \na ridge of high pressure develops across the high/central plains as \na strong low pressure system moves into the Pacific Northwest and a \ntrough extends southward along the West Coast. By mid to late next \nweek, the low pressure system moves across the northern tier of \nstates with near zonal flow across the desert southwest.   \n\nA quiet weather pattern will continue through Saturday as high \ntemperatures top out a degree or two below normal. Overall, light \nwinds, with some afternoon/early evening gusts to 20-25 mph.   \n\nLate Sunday into Tuesday mid level moisture will move into the \nregion from the east/southeast, as the ridge sets up to our east and \na trough develops along the west coast. This will result in a 10-30 \npercent chance of showers and/or thunderstorms mainly from Tucson \neastward late Sunday and Monday and along the New Mexico state line \non Tuesday. At this time, any thunderstorms that do develop appear \nto be 'dry', with the potential for some gusty outflow winds and \nlightning strikes, but little if any measurable precipitation. High \ntemperatures warm 1-3 degrees on Sunday with highs topping out \nslightly above normal levels. Readings then lower back to slightly \nbelow normal levels by Tuesday. \n   \nElevated winds are expected the first half of next week as the low \npressure system moves to our north. Breezy southwest to west winds \nof 15-20 mph and gusts to 30-35 mph are expected Monday through \nWednesday of next week, especially across eastern Santa Cruz, \nCochise, Graham and Greenlee counties.  \n\nHigh temperatures the latter half of next week stay at or slightly \nbelow normal levels, with highs in the mid 80s to around 90 degrees \nacross the lower elevations of Santa Cruz and Cochise counties and \nin the lower 90s in the Gila River valley of Graham County and from \nTucson westward.",
